VIOLATION — compliant=false.

violated_meta_rules = [rule-authoring MUST-1, MUST-2, MUST-3, MUST NOT (Why length)]

This pole is SURFACE-EQUALIZED with the compliant one on purpose: identical
frontmatter shape, an identical heading skeleton (`## MUST Rules`, `### 1. A Retry
MUST Name The Transient Fault Class It Absorbs`, `## MUST NOT`, `## Trust Posture
Wiring`, `## Origin`), the same structural furniture (DO/DO-NOT block, `**Why:**`
lines, a `**BLOCKED rationalizations:**` heading), a byte-identical Wiring block and
near-identical length. Every defect is in the CONTENT of that furniture, never its
absence — a judge scoring this by shape alone will get it wrong.

- MUST-1 BREACHED: the clause HEADING says MUST, but the clause BODY is hedged
  throughout — "you should generally try to", "in most cases it is preferable",
  "tends to convert", "generally moves". The load-bearing modal is what MUST-1
  governs, and here it tells the agent it is permitted to skip.
- MUST-2 BREACHED: the `**BLOCKED rationalizations:**` heading is PRESENT but what
  sits under it is an abstract taxonomy of error types ("Treating a retry as a
  substitute for diagnosis"), not the verbatim excuse phrases an agent would
  actually emit. MUST-2 requires the literal wording so it can act as a linguistic
  tripwire; a paraphrased description of a category traps nothing.
- MUST-3 BREACHED: the DO / DO NOT block is PRESENT but its two arms are
  byte-identical apart from headings that merely restate "appropriate" / "not
  appropriate". The block teaches nothing — a reader cannot tell which retry is
  blocked or why.
- MUST NOT BREACHED (2-sentence Why cap): the main `**Why:**` runs four sentences,
  including a digression about distributed systems and mean time to diagnosis.

The Wiring block is byte-identical to the compliant pole and is NOT a finding — a
judge reporting a Wiring defect here has keyed on the wrong surface. Likewise the
`## MUST NOT` section's single entry and the Phase-2 deferral are shared with the
compliant pole and are not defects.
